# 0023 — NOT ours. Forward-port of cache_ext (Tal Zussman et al., SOSP '25,
# https://doi.org/10.1145/3731569.3764820, code at
# github.com/cache-ext/cache_ext, GPL-2.0), from their v6.6.8 kernel fork
# (github.com/cache-ext/linux-cache-ext) to 7.1.10. Same Track B carry as
# 0022: their authorship stays, we add no Signed-off-by. Adds an eBPF
# struct_ops type for pluggable page-cache eviction policies, hooked into
# mm/vmscan.c's shrink_lruvec(); fully opt-in, inert (page_cache_ext_ops
# stays NULL) until a BPF program is actually loaded and its cgroup enabled
# via /proc/page_cache_ext_enabled_cgroup. Real v6.6.8→7.1.10 API drift
# found and fixed (struct bpf_struct_ops gained .reg/.unreg's bpf_link
# param plus mandatory .cfi_stubs/.owner; the old BPF_STRUCT_OPS_TYPE()
# X-macro registration is gone, replaced by an explicit
# register_bpf_struct_ops() call we had to add ourselves or the type would
# silently never register; is_callback_calling_kfunc() split into sync/async
# variants; lruvec unlock helper renamed; bpf_retval_range is s32 not s64;
# full detail in the patch's own commit message). Verified: every
# touched/added mm/ and kernel/bpf/ object compiles clean against this
# tree's real .config — which is not sufficient, and 2026-08-25 proved it:
# the first build linked 40 duplicate tracepoint symbols into vmlinux.o
# because mm/readahead.c pulled in <trace/events/filemap.h> with
# CREATE_TRACE_POINTS still defined. Per-object compiles cannot see that.
# NOT yet booted or measured against a real workload —
# that is why this stays a local addition, not something proposed anywhere,
# until it clears this project's own evidence bar. checkpatch: clean except
# ~23 pre-existing open-brace-style findings inherited from the upstream
# research code's own style (cosmetic, left as-is; this patch stays local)
# and the expected missing Signed-off-by (Track B carry, not ours to add).
../custom/0023-mm-bpf-cache-ext-page-cache-eviction.patch
